<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antioch</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antioch was an ancient city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes River</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a>, it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> and later one of the great cities of the Roman East. It was captured more than once by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, notably in 540.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antioch.jpg" alt="Antioch" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UndercoverClassicist · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antioch_on_the_Orontes_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Some Armenian leaders set themselves up as lords; the most successful, the former Byzantine general Philaretos Brachamios, held between 1078 and 1085 a principality from Malatia to <b>Antioch</b> and from Cilicia to Edessa; it crumbled before his death in 1090.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He fought at <b>Antioch</b> and Jerusalem, and from 1102 besieged Tripoli, building the castle of Mont Pèlerin outside it; he died there in 1105, and the city fell in 1109 to his son Bertrand, founding the County of Tripoli.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hauteville brothers Robert Guiscard and Roger I conquered southern Italy and Sicily between the 1040s and 1091, Roger II was crowned King of Sicily in 1130, and Bohemond of Taranto founded the Principality of <b>Antioch</b> during the First Crusade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After taking <b>Antioch</b> in 1098, the crusaders reached Jerusalem, then held by the Fatimid Caliphate, and stormed it on 15 July 1099, massacring much of its Muslim and Jewish population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexandria, and later successor foundations such as <b>Antioch</b> along with older cities such as Pergamon, became centres of commerce, scholarship and culture.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In return the crusader princes granted Genoa quarters, tax exemptions and a share of revenues in Acre, <b>Antioch</b> and other ports.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
